null-control lift K=11
PASS  NULL-CONTROL-GETS-AT-LEAST-GI-ONS-SHADOW-FLOOR  — null p10=15.641 vs gi-on p10=15.001: the control was given 0.640 luma MORE shadow lift than the fix bought. Must be non-negative (the control is never short-changed) and within one 8-bit quantum (1.0 luma) so it is not handed an unearned advantage either. THIS CHECK WAS FOUND FAILING BY RUNNING IT: it originally demanded a match within 0.5 luma, which an integer-quantised lift can never deliver — measured p10 4.734 -> 15.734 at K=11 against a target of 15.116. That was the check being wrong about arithmetic, not the control being wrong about lighting.
PASS  ALGEBRAIC-LIFT-COMPRESSES-RATIO  — measured null falloff_ratio=4.2006 vs the algebraic prediction (p90+K)/(p10+K)=4.2007 — confirms the pixel-level measurement matches the closed-form identity (informational: this identity holds regardless of which arm "wins" on the ratio itself, which is why the ratio is not the gating check)
PASS  GI-MOVES-THE-LIT-END-FAR-LESS-THAN-A-MATCHED-GLOBAL-LIFT  — for the SAME shadow-floor lift (p10 matched to within one 8-bit quantum), gi-on moves p90_luma by 0.155 while the null control — which by construction of an additive lift moves EVERY pixel by exactly K=11.00 — moves it by 11.000. This is the real directional-falloff claim: a spatially-local fix can leave an already-lit surface's brightness alone while raising a shadow floor by the same amount; a flat lift structurally cannot touch one region without touching all of them.
PASS  GLOBAL-LIFT-COMPRESSES-BELOW-GI-OFF-BASELINE  — null-control falloff_ratio=4.2006 vs the untouched gi-off baseline=11.7861 — any positive flat lift strictly compresses the ratio below where it started (the algebraic identity again; kept as an honest, if non-gating, fact about global lifts)
